= Solution
{parent=i}

Suppose $F:\mathbb D\to\mathbb C$ were a <holomorphic> bijection. By the allowed nonvanishing derivative result, $F'(z)\ne0$ throughout $\mathbb D$. The <holomorphic inverse function theorem> gives a <holomorphic> inverse locally at every image point. Since $F$ is globally bijective, these local inverses agree with its global inverse $H:\mathbb C\to\mathbb D$.

Thus $H$ is an <entire function> with $|H|<1$. The <Liouville theorem> forces $H$ to be constant, contradicting its being an inverse. Therefore
$$
\boxed{\text{there is no holomorphic bijection }\mathbb D\to\mathbb C}.
$$
This is the analytic obstruction behind the fact that the <complex plane and unit disc are homeomorphic but not conformally equivalent>.

= ii
{parent=c}
{c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Yes. An explicit <holomorphic> surjection is the <rational function>
$$
\boxed{F(z)=\left(\frac z{1-z}\right)^2,\qquad |z|<1}.
$$
Its denominator never vanishes inside the <unit disc>. To prove surjectivity, take any $w\in\mathbb C$ and choose a square root $r$ of $w$ with $\operatorname{Re}r\geq0$; one of the two opposite roots always has this property. Define $z=r/(1+r)$. Then $1+r\ne0$ and
$$
|1+r|^2-|r|^2=1+2\operatorname{Re}r>0,
$$
so $|z|<1$. Also $z/(1-z)=r$, and consequently $F(z)=r^2=w$.

This is a <holomorphic surjection from the unit disc onto the complex plane>. It does not contradict part (i): it is not injective. For example, $F(i/(1+i))=F(-i/(1-i))=-1$, with two distinct arguments of modulus $1/\sqrt2$.
